\section{Why \code{excess_returns} subtracts from the data rather than the moments}{

The obvious way to make a maximum-Sharpe objective use excess returns is a
custom \code{momentFUN} returning \code{mu = colMeans(R) - rf}. It does not work, and it
fails quietly. \code{optimize.portfolio()} finds the target return that maximises
\code{mean / sd} using that \code{mu}, then sets \code{moments$mean <- rep(0, N)} before the
final solve; \code{gmv_opt()} sees an all-zero mean vector, reads it as "no mean
was supplied", and falls back to \code{colMeans(R)} — the \strong{total} means — when it
imposes \verb{mu'w == target}. The target is chosen on one frontier and imposed on
another.

Measured on the 16 risky class series with a 60-month window: the \code{momentFUN}
route lands up to 0.29 in weight away from the exact long-only tangency
portfolio and loses up to 0.05 of in-sample Sharpe ratio, while subtracting
the rate from the returns reproduces the exact tangency to within 0.03 in
weight and four decimals of Sharpe ratio. Hence \code{excess_returns}, not
\code{excess_moments}.
}

\section{Why a strategy carries its own metric}{

A conditional value-at-risk optimisation reported only by its Sharpe ratio is
a misreport. It was not asked to maximise a Sharpe ratio, it was asked to
minimise a tail, and the number that says whether it did its job is the tail.
Declaring the metric alongside the specification means
\code{\link[=pm_backtest_metrics]{pm_backtest_metrics()}} can pull the matching one for every strategy in a
register at once, instead of a fixed column that flatters one objective and
libels another. The full table is still there in \verb{$metrics} for anyone who
wants to make the comparison anyway — deliberately, and with the mismatch in
plain sight.

When \code{metric} is \code{NULL} it is inferred from the enabled objectives of the
specification: an \code{ES}, \code{ETL} or \code{CVaR} objective gives \code{"es_95"}; \code{maxSR = TRUE}, or a \code{mean} and a \code{StdDev} objective together, gives \code{"sharpe"}; a
\code{StdDev}, \code{sd} or \code{var} objective alone gives \code{"ann_vol"}; a \code{mean} objective
alone gives \code{"ann_return"}; no objective at all gives \code{"sharpe"}.
}

\examples{
# Minimum variance, long only. The spec is a function of the universe, so the
# same declaration runs at class level and at instrument level.
min_var <- pm_strategy(
  "min_variance",
  spec = function(assets) {
    p <- PortfolioAnalytics::portfolio.spec(assets)
    p <- PortfolioAnalytics::add.constraint(p, "full_investment")
    p <- PortfolioAnalytics::add.constraint(p, "long_only")
    PortfolioAnalytics::add.objective(p, type = "risk", name = "StdDev")
  },
  optimize_method = "ROI",
  label = "Minimum variance"
)
min_var

# A rule that needs no optimiser still carries a spec, because
# equal.weight() reads max_sum and the asset names off it.
one_over_n <- pm_strategy(
  "equal_weight",
  spec = function(assets) {
    p <- PortfolioAnalytics::portfolio.spec(assets)
    p <- PortfolioAnalytics::add.constraint(p, "full_investment")
    PortfolioAnalytics::add.constraint(p, "long_only")
  },
  weight_fun = PortfolioAnalytics::equal.weight,
  metric = "sharpe",
  label = "Equal weight"
)

reg <- pm_register(one_over_n, min_var)
reg
names(reg)

}
